{
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The vehicle did leave the recommended route, and the rider's account of the streets is corroborated on this trip's own record: the trip record logs route_deviation_detected at 08:40:24 ('recommended Tampines Expressway (TPE), vehicle took Tampines Avenue 7') and route_rejoined at 08:48:22, and the route actually driven runs via Tampines Avenue 7 and Simei Avenue while the recommended line runs via the TPE and the PIE. The rider was taken through Tampines and Simei, not along the recommended route, and POL-RD-22 makes the GPS trace the evidence of which route was taken and when.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-TRIP-05018", "EV-GPS-05018"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-22"],
      "relies_on": "trip_record.events",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The driver gave the rider no notice of the deviation at all. The trip's chat log is a complete, contiguous log carrying 0 messages, and the trip record's in_app_calls list is empty, so nothing was sent in-app 'before or at the point of deviation' as POL-RD-21 requires; POL-RD-21 also provides that a verbal notice with no recording cannot be established, and there is no call recording in this record. The deviation therefore cannot be treated as one the rider agreed to, and the rider was given no opportunity to object while the driver was off the recommended route.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-CHAT-05018", "EV-TRIP-05018"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-21", "POL-GEN-26"],
      "relies_on": "chat_log.messages",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The driver's explanation does not survive the traffic data. The LTA advisory covers the very segment he blames - road 'Tampines Expressway (TPE)', location 'Tampines Avenue 7 to PIE', direction 'towards PIE' - and it overlaps the trip window, yet its type is NONE and its message is 'No incident, roadwork or closure recorded for the queried segment and window. Speed bands 4-5 throughout.' POL-RD-23 justifies a reroute only where such data shows congestion, an incident or a closure, and expressly says a driver's assertion of traffic with no such data does not justify the reroute. The exit at Tampines Avenue 7 onto Tampines Avenue 7 was not a justified reroute, and the driver's post-hoc statement offers nothing but the assertion POL-RD-23 rejects.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-ADVIS-05018-01", "EV-TRIP-05018"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-23"],
      "relies_on": "traffic_advisory.derived.justifies_reroute",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The rider is still paying for that detour. The fare ledger shows payment_status CHARGED and refund_issued_sgd 0.00 on a metered fare of 19.64 SGD, and the record's own excess-distance arithmetic is 0.80 km x 0.72 SGD per km = 0.58 SGD, without surge, which is POL-RD-24's formula for the excess distance fare. On POL-GEN-03's definition, NO_ACTION preserves exactly that state and leaves the rider carrying distance the driver added and neither justified under POL-RD-23 nor disclosed under POL-RD-21.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-FARE-05018", "EV-TRIP-05018"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-24", "POL-RD-21", "POL-GEN-03"],
      "relies_on": "fare_breakdown.derived.money.excess_distance_fare_sgd",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    },
    {
      "text": "The open question on this record is notice, and it should not be closed against the rider. There is no chat entry and no call recording, and POL-RD-21 treats an unrecorded verbal notice as something that cannot be established, so whether the driver met his disclosure obligation is unresolved; the recorded deviation of 0.80 km sits within POL-RD-22's 1.0 km tolerance, and the case should not be shut on arithmetic that walks past the unresolved question. POL-GEN-26 requires the case to be escalated where a fact a rule needs cannot be established, and under POL-GEN-03 an escalated case holds the disputed 5.00 SGD rather than leaving it collected.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-CHAT-05018", "EV-FARE-05018", "EV-TRIP-05018"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-GEN-26", "POL-RD-21", "POL-GEN-03"],
      "relies_on": "chat_log.message_count",
      "asserted_weight": 1.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "ruling": {
    "ruling": "NO_ACTION",
    "amount_sgd": null,
    "driver_compensation_sgd": null,
    "referrals": [],
    "confidence": 0.9,
    "explanation": "This is a route-deviation dispute filed by the rider, who says the driver took a roughly 4 km detour through Tampines and Simei and asks for 5.00 SGD back. The ruling is NO_ACTION: the rider's claim is not supported and the present state stands, with no money moved.\n\nWhy. POL-RD-22 measures the deviation as the platform's recorded actual distance minus its recorded optimal distance, not from the sparse GPS trace, because a sparse trace understates a route's length. On EV-TRIP-05018 those recorded distances are 12.8 km actual against 12.0 km optimal, a deviation of 0.80 km (6.7%), and 0.80 km is at or below POL-RD-22's 1.0 km tolerance. Almost none of the rider's asserted detour survives: the trace (EV-GPS-05018, 41 fixes at 30 s, no gaps) shows one excursion off the recommended line — departing at the TPE / Tampines Avenue 7 exit at 08:40:24 and rejoining at Simei Avenue / Changi Business Park Avenue 1 at 08:48:22 — not a 4 km diversion.\n\nThe refund matrix in POL-RD-24 turns on two things: whether the reroute was traffic-justified, and whether the deviation was over 1 km. The LTA advisory covering the very segment the driver blames (EV-ADVIS-05018-01: road TPE, location 'Tampines Avenue 7 to PIE', type NONE, 'No incident, roadwork or closure recorded', speed bands 4-5) does not show congestion, an incident or a closure, so POL-RD-23 says the reroute was not traffic-justified; the driver's own statement that the TPE exit 'got queue' is the bare assertion POL-RD-23 rejects. That puts the case in POL-RD-24 row (e) — not traffic-justified, deviation 1 km or less — which returns no refund, within tolerance. The driver's own framing (row (c), the mirror-image tolerance row) reaches the same answer, so exactly one row applies.\n\nOn the rider's points: it is true the driver did not notify the rider of the deviation — the chat log EV-CHAT-05018 carries 0 messages and the trip record's in-app call list is empty, so POL-RD-21's disclosure duty was not met and any verbal notice cannot be established on this record. That fact does not move the outcome here: POL-RD-24 makes notification decisive only in rows (a) and (b), both of which require a deviation over 1 km, and a within-tolerance deviation returns no refund whether or not the rider was told. The rider's advocate's own arithmetic — 0.80 km x 0.72 SGD per km = 0.58 SGD — is the most the matrix could ever pay on this trip, and it is payable only where the deviation exceeds 1 km; the written-down excess-distance fare is 0.58 SGD. The rider's request for 5.00 SGD is therefore well beyond any row. There is no POL-GEN-26 escalation either: the deviation distance is established by the recorded distances, the traffic justification is established by the advisory, and the one fact not affirmatively established (a recorded verbal notice) is a fact the applicable rows do not require, so nothing a rule needs is left open. The rider's suggestion that the driver 'does it to everyone' cannot be used — POL-GEN-01 makes every profile field except fraud_flags and no_show_cancellations_30d context only, and none of those fields may establish a fact about this trip.\n\nWho decides. The contested amount is 5.00 SGD, below POL-GEN-22's 10.00 SGD band, so the AI ruling is final at any confidence; neither profile carries a fraud flag (fraud_flags 0), so no 0.95 threshold applies; and the 0.80 km deviation is under POL-RD-25's 2 km limit, so the AI may rule rather than escalate. Confidence in this ruling is 0.9, above the 0.80 POL-RD-25 requires; every artifact relied on (EV-TRIP-05018, EV-GPS-05018, EV-ADVIS-05018-01, EV-CHAT-05018, EV-FARE-05018) carries weight_multiplier 1.0 with no issue codes. No referral clause applies to a route deviation in this version, so none is issued."
  },
  "discarded_claims": []
}
